From 00a445d733086da52c6b905fa287f0ea64a85938 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 23 May 2026 16:39:59 -0700
Subject: [PATCH RFC 0/3] net: flow_dissector: 5-tuple-only hash helper + sch_cake adoption

Hi all,

This RFC adds flow_hash_from_keys_small() to net/core/flow_dissector.c
as a 5-tuple-only companion to flow_hash_from_keys(), documents the
flow_keys consumer landscape, and adopts the new helper in sch_cake's
host-load accounting hashes.

Background — first kernel submission
====================================

This is my first patch submission to the Linux kernel, so a
few words of context up front.

I contribute to XDP2, the flow-dissection / parser-codegen
project led by Tom Herbert (the project formerly known as
PANDA; see "Replacing Flow Dissector with PANDA Parser",
Netdev 0x15 https://netdevconf.info/0x15/slides/16/Flow%20dissector_PANDA%20parser.pdf
and the 0x16 follow-up). XDP2 explores a codegen-based
replacement for the kernel's __skb_flow_dissect()
infrastructure — a larger architectural direction Tom and
team have been pursuing for years.

In the course of comparing XDP2's generated parsers against
the kernel's flow_dissector + skb_get_hash path, I ended up
auditing how the kernel actually consumes struct flow_keys
today. The audit (data: https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2
branch flow-keys-compat-reorder, directory
perf-results/2026-05-23-flow-keys-consumer-audit/) surfaced
the observation behind this series: flow_hash_from_keys()
hashes a larger region than any consumer actually reads at
the field level, and a 5-tuple-only variant gives the same
RSS distribution at lower cost.

This series is explicitly NOT the XDP2 wholesale replacement —
it is a small, narrowly-scoped helper that ADDS one function,
doesn't change any existing call path, doesn't touch any
hash-stability guarantee (skb->hash, RPS/RFS, multipath
routing all unchanged), and adopts itself in exactly one
consumer (sch_cake's host_keys accounting). I think it's
small enough to be safe and the measurements show a
consistent (if uarch-dependent) improvement. It's the
incremental win I noticed while doing the broader comparison.

Sent as RFC because (a) I'm new to the kernel review
process and would value feedback before a non-RFC posting,
and (b) the helper name and v6 handling in patch 2 are
worth bike-shedding through before the formal submission.

Motivation
==========

Auditing nine kernel flow_keys consumers (sch_cake, sch_choke,
cls_flow, ipv4/route, ipv6/route, fib_frontend, ipv4/netfilter,
ipv6/netfilter, flow_dissector itself) shows that the canonical
5-tuple (basic.ip_proto + ports + addrs + control.addr_type) is
the highest-touch field set. The fields between them in the
flow_keys hash region (tags.flow_label, vlan, cvlan, keyid,
icmp) are read by no consumer's logic directly; they only
contribute to flow_hash_from_keys() output.

For consumers that own both endpoints of the hash comparison
(e.g. sch_cake's internal host-load counters), hashing those
"hash-only" fields is wasted cycles for zero entropy gain.

Measurements
============

Userspace microbench of siphash() on two AMD uarches:
  - Zen 2: Ryzen Threadripper PRO 3945WX
  - Zen 1: Ryzen 5 PRO 2400G (two hosts, hp2 and hp5;
    same SKU)

p50 cycles, including rdtsc/cpuid fence overhead (~40 cyc
Zen 2, ~36 cyc Zen 1):

  region size   Zen 2 p50   Zen 1 p50
  -----------   ---------   ---------
       16 B          80         108
       24 B          80         108
       32 B          80         108
       40 B         120         108   (band layout differs)
       48 B         120         108
       56 B         120         144
       64 B         120         144
       72 B         120         144

Full flow_hash_from_keys():

  variant   region    Zen 2 p50   Zen 1 p50
  -------   ------    ---------   ---------
  IPv4      40 B          120         108
  IPv6      64 B          160         144

5-tuple-only hash (16 B v4):
  Zen 2 p50 = 80   (1 band saved over current v4)
  Zen 1 p50 = 108  (same band as current v4 at p50)
  Zen 1 p10 = 72   (saving visible at p10 only — see notes)

Headline: on Zen 2, IPv4 hash is ~50% faster at median.
On Zen 1, the v4 saving is masked by measurement floor at
median, but visible at p10 (the underlying delta is ~36 cyc,
just below the rdtsc resolution at p50). IPv6 hash saves on
both uarches (~40 cyc Zen 2, ~36 cyc Zen 1) because the v6
region is 64 B and crosses a siphash band on both.

No uarch shows a regression. The size of the win depends on
uarch, percentile, and address family.

Chi-squared distribution test (100k synthetic IPv4 5-tuples,
hash bucketed into K=256 and K=4096):
  K=256:  full=242.5  vs  small=260.6  (df=255)
  K=4096: full=4070.5 vs small=4155.3  (df=4095)

Both bucket counts land squarely in the expected uniform range
(df +/- sqrt(2*df)). The smaller region is statistically
equivalent for RSS/RFS distribution purposes — as expected
since siphash is a PRF whose distribution quality is
algorithmically independent of input length above one
absorption block. Reproduced bit-exactly on both Zen 1 and
Zen 2 (same algorithm, deterministic).

Sanity check on real traffic: ran the same chi-squared
against IPv4 TCP/UDP flows extracted from a small protocol-
coverage pcap (360 flows, broad-coverage.pcap). Both regions
gave chi2 in the expected-uniform range for that sample size
and were within 1.5 of each other at K=256 and K=4096. The
small-pcap result is consistent with the synthetic uniform
result; a larger real-traffic capture would tighten the
confidence interval.

Patches
=======

  1/3  Documentation/networking: add flow_dissector consumer
       touch matrix (NEW docs file + index.rst entry)

  2/3  net: flow_dissector: add flow_hash_from_keys_small()
       5-tuple helper (~100 LoC; new opt-in API; doesn't change
       existing call paths)

  3/3  net: sched: sch_cake: use flow_hash_from_keys_small()
       for host accounting (~8 line change). Per-packet
       saving depends on uarch and traffic mix: ~80 cyc/pkt
       on Zen 2 v4, ~0-72 cyc/pkt on Zen 1 v4 depending on
       percentile, ~72 cyc/pkt on Zen 1 v6 / ~80 cyc/pkt on
       Zen 2 v6. Never a regression. The change is small
       enough that even modest savings compound on busy
       queues; net-positive even in the worst Zen 1 v4-only
       case where the median is unchanged. The recently
       merged cake_mq qdisc auto-installs an sch_cake per
       HW TX queue and routes each instance through the same
       cake_hash() code; the saving therefore scales
       linearly with NIC queue count on cake_mq setups.

What this is NOT
================

- NOT a flow_keys struct layout change. The struct is
  ABI-affecting (BPF flow_dissector hook contract); this patch
  series only adds a new helper.
- NOT bit-exact with flow_hash_from_keys(). The new helper
  produces different hash values; callers that need the RSS
  hash must continue using the existing helper.
- NOT a sch_cake main-flow-hash change. Patch 3 switches only
  the two host_keys hashes; the cake-internal queue-index hash
  remains on flow_hash_from_keys() for minimal review surface.

Prior art
=========

The "hash a subset of flow_keys" pattern already exists in
__skb_get_hash_symmetric_net() (commit predates 2019; David
Miller, for PACKET_FANOUT symmetric hashing). That function
uses a separate flow_keys_dissector_symmetric that populates
only proto+addrs+ports, then siphash-hashes the full flow_keys
region (the cold fields contribute zero entropy because they
are zero). Our helper is conceptually similar but takes a
populated flow_keys and hashes only the populated bytes — same
effective entropy, fewer siphash rounds.

We did not find any prior posting on lore.kernel.org / LKML
proposing a smaller-region variant of flow_hash_from_keys()
for performance. The siphash conversion (55667441c84f, Oct
2019) was security-driven and did not reconsider the region
size. The PANDA / Flow Dissector replacement effort (Tom
Herbert, Pedro Tammela, Netdev 0x15/0x16) aimed at wholesale
replacement; it has not been merged. This patch is the
incremental win that the wholesale-replacement plan made
nobody bother to propose.

Why __skb_get_hash_symmetric_net() is not the right place for
this: that function re-runs the dissector on the skb to
populate its own flow_keys. Our callers already have a
populated flow_keys from a prior skb_flow_dissect_flow_keys()
call (sch_cake dissects once per packet, then hashes three
times). We need the post-dissect hash to be cheap.

Blast radius
============

The patch series touches exactly one consumer (sch_cake's
two host_keys hashes; 4 call sites total). It leaves
unchanged:

- __skb_get_hash_net() and therefore skb->hash population
- get_rps_cpu() and the RPS/RFS steering chain
- fib_multipath_hash_from_keys() and all 11 IPv{4,6}/route.c
  ECMP call sites
- sch_cake's main flow_hash (kept on flow_hash_from_keys()
  because it interacts with skb->hash via the use_skbhash
  shortcut)
- __skb_get_hash_symmetric_net() (independent path)
- All NIC ARFS driver code (11+ drivers; none call
  flow_hash_from_keys())
- The BPF flow_dissector hook ABI (struct layout unchanged)

Every hash-stability path stays bit-stable.

Related — separate future submission
====================================

While doing the XDP2-vs-kernel comparison, we also noticed
that the in-tree BPF flow_dissector reference at
tools/testing/selftests/bpf/progs/bpf_flow.c does not handle
PPPoE session packets (ETH_P_PPP_SES, 0x8864) — the _dissect()
entry point branches on n_proto for IP / IPv6 / VLAN /
MPLS variants but has no PPP_SES case, so PPPoE-encapsulated
flows get dropped by that reference program. A ~50-line
patch adding ETH_P_PPP_SES dispatch and a small PPP
sub-program would close that gap.

That fix is out of scope for this series (different file,
different maintainer audience, different test surface) and
will be sent as a separate patch set after this one settles.
Mentioning it here only because the BPF folks may see this
series and recognise the same comparison work that turned up
the PPPoE finding.

Pre-post checklist done
=======================

Static + build verification:

- checkpatch.pl --strict passes (0 errors, 0 warnings, 0 checks)
  for all three patches.
- net/core/flow_dissector.o + net/sched/sch_cake.o build clean
  (gcc 15.2.0).
- Full vmlinux + modules + bzImage build via NixOS flake
  (linuxKernel.manualConfig) — across two different nixpkgs
  pins, both produce identical artifact hashes.

Microbench (Phase 3 measurements):

- Cycle measurements on FOUR Zen 1 hosts (hp1, hp2, hp3, hp5;
  all Ryzen 5 PRO 2400G) AND one Zen 2 (Threadripper PRO 3945WX).
  Two uarches give different band layouts; the patch is
  net-positive on both. Hash bands reproduce identically across
  all four Zen 1 hosts down to the digit — confirms cycle bands
  are CPU-property, not NIC- or testbed-specific.
- Chi-squared distribution test (100k synthetic uniform IPv4
  5-tuples): full vs small region statistically indistinguishable
  at K=256 (chi2 242.5 vs 260.6) and K=4096 (4070.5 vs 4155.3).
- Real-traffic chi-squared on broad-coverage.pcap (360 IPv4
  TCP/UDP flows): both regions pass uniformity, within 1.5 chi2
  of each other.

Runtime validation on patched kernel (BOOTED 7.1.0-rc4-flowdis-pppoe):

- Built the patched kernel as a NixOS flake derivation and
  booted on all FOUR testbed hosts. All came back via SSH
  in 1-2 minutes; no console intervention needed.
- /proc/kallsyms shows flow_hash_from_keys_small + _seed
  exported on all four hosts.
- sch_cake.ko correctly references both flow_hash_from_keys()
  and flow_hash_from_keys_small() as unresolved symbols (the
  patch-3 substitution is in the object code, not just source).
- Functional sch_cake test on all four hosts:
  triple-isolate mode (= exercises patched host_keys hashes)
  on a dummy interface, real ICMP traffic, classes populate
  correctly, no drops, no kernel WARN/BUG/oops in dmesg.
- 25 GbE stress test: iperf3 hp1 (gen) -> hp3 (DUT) with
  16 parallel TCP flows over 60 s through patched cake
  triple-isolate on enp1s0f0np0. 16.5 Gbit/s sustained,
  124 GB transferred, zero drops at the cake qdisc, host-
  accounting classes populated; patched flow_hash_from_keys_small
  exercised under real high-bandwidth load with no
  regressions.
- tdc.py cake.json: tests 1-3 PASS (setup/teardown/config).
  Tests 4+ blocked by a NixOS test-harness dummy-interface
  lifecycle issue unrelated to our patch (same blocker would
  hit the unpatched 7.0.1 baseline kernel).

Multi-NIC + multi-link-rate coverage:

  host  NIC                            link    role
  ----  -----------------------------  ------  ----
  hp1   Mellanox ConnectX-4 Lx mlx5    25 GbE  gen
  hp3   Mellanox ConnectX-4 Lx mlx5    25 GbE  DUT
  hp2   Intel X710 i40e                10 GbE  gen
  hp5   Intel X710 i40e                10 GbE  DUT

  All four booted on the patched kernel; both NIC drivers
  (i40e and mlx5_core) loaded correctly; bidirectional ping
